Chapter: KeyProof

 It draws no text and loads no font, because the instrument must not depend on
 the subsystem it is meant to report on: on the ASUS TUF the text renderer is
 currently wrong, and a probe that answers in glyphs answers in the one
 language that panel cannot currently speak. This one answers in whole-screen
 colour, which survives any font, scale, stride or layout defect.

 It calls uefi-read-key-ex, which is the only input path in the tree that asks
 the firmware for a key. The path everything else uses, uefi-read-key, is four
 instructions that swap a value out of the PS/2 key cell filled by the IRQ1
 handler from port 0x60. The target board has no PS/2 port, so that path cannot
 deliver a key on it however correct the USB stack is.

 It does not call ExitBootServices. Boot services own ConIn, and a payload that
 exits them before reading a key has thrown away the working keyboard to go and
 build one.

 Colours: blue means running and waiting. Green means a key arrived. Red means
 the firmware refused the InputEx protocol, which is a different answer from
 "no key" and must look different.

  cites Works chapter GopHandoff
  cites Works chapter GopDraw

Section: Proof

  kp-blue : Integer = 2105408
  kp-green : Integer = 1073920
  kp-red : Integer = 8388608
  kp-yellow : Integer = 16776960

 uefi-read-key-ex answers -1 for BOTH "the SystemTable snapshot cell is zero"
 and "LocateProtocol refused", which are different faults with different fixes.
 uefi-systab-addr is cell 36208, filled by emit-start from whatever the booting
 stub left at 0x8000 -- and the two stubs disagree about what lives there, so a
 zero is a stub problem and not a firmware one. Reading it directly is what
 makes the two answers look different.

  kp-systab : Integer -> Integer
  kp-systab (dummy) = peek-qword 36208 0

  kp-magenta : Integer = 16711935

 A CONTROL ARM, and without it a red result cannot be attributed. uefi-read-key-ex
 failing tells us nothing on its own: it could be InputEx specifically, or it
 could be that NO call into firmware survives emit-start loading CR3 with our
 own page tables. uefi-clear-screen reaches firmware the same way -- systab,
 ConOut at +64, call -- so if it fails too the fault is structural and the
 keyboard is not the subject at all. Magenta means firmware calls are dead
 generally; that is a different bug with a different fix.

  kp-fw : Integer -> Integer
  kp-fw (dummy) = uefi-clear-screen

 uefi-read-key-ex answers 0 for "no key yet" and a negative value when the
 protocol could not be located, so the two failures are distinguishable.

 uefi-read-key-ex answers -1 for EFI_NOT_READY as well as for a genuine
 failure, and EFI_NOT_READY is simply "no key pending", which is the normal
 answer on almost every poll. So a loop that treats a negative as fatal exits
 on its first iteration having waited for nothing. Only a POSITIVE answer is a
 key; everything else means keep asking until the fuel runs out.

 The fuel has to be small enough that the loop ENDS inside the screenshot
 window. At 60 million it never did, so every run photographed the initial blue
 and "still polling" was the only answer the instrument could give -- which is
 not an answer. Two million finishes in a few seconds, and the terminal colour
 reports the LAST value seen rather than merely that we were still asking:
 red for negative (error or NOT_READY), cyan for zero, green for a real key.

  kp-cyan : Integer = 65535

  kp-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  kp-loop (base) (w) (h) (stride) (last) =
    let k = uefi-read-key-ex
    in let cat = if k == 0 then 2 else if k == -1 then 1 else 3
    in if cat == last then kp-loop base w h stride last
    else let col = if cat == 3 then kp-green else if cat == 4 then kp-magenta else if cat == 2 then kp-cyan else kp-red
      in let f = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h col
      in if cat == 3 then 3 else if cat == 4 then 4
      else kp-loop base w h stride cat

  opening : Nothing -> [Console] Nothing
  opening (dummy) =
    let base = boot-fb-base 0
    in let w = boot-fb-w 0
    in let h = boot-fb-h 0
    in let stride = boot-fb-stride 0
    in let fw = kp-fw 0
    in let start = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h kp-blue
    in let done = if kp-systab 0 == 0
      then gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h kp-yellow
      else if fw < 0 then gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h kp-magenta
      else kp-loop base w h stride 0
    in print-line "keyproof done"
